-- title:
--   Coset graph at a split prime is the Bruhat–Tits tree
-- statement:
--   Let $a,b\in\mathbb{Q}$ and let $R$ be a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule of $\mathbb{H}=\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q},a,b]$ satisfying `IsOrder`: $1\in R$, $R$ is closed under multiplication, its $\mathbb{Q}$-span is all of $\mathbb{H}$, and it is finitely generated. Let $r$ be a prime number, $v$ a height-one prime of $\mathcal{O}_{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $r\in v$, and $\varphi:\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{Q}_v\to M_2(\mathbb{Q}_v)$ a ring isomorphism such that $\varphi(1\otimes t)=t\cdot 1$ for all $t\in\mathbb{Q}_v$, and such that an element $x$ lies in [`Submodule.localBox R v`](def/Submodule_LocalBox.html#L40) — the additive subgroup generated by the tensors $z\otimes c$ with $z\in R$ and $c$ in the valuation ring of $\mathbb{Q}_v$ — exactly when all four entries $\varphi(x)_{ij}$ are integral. Let $n$ be a unit of $\mathbb{H}\otimes_{\mathbb{Q}}\mathbb{A}_{\mathbb{Q}}^{\mathrm{fin}}$ lying in `primeHeckeSet R r`, i.e. $n$ and $r\cdot n^{-1}$ lie in the adelic box of $R$ while $n^{-1}$ and $r^{-1}\cdot n$ do not. Write $n_v$ for the image of $n$ under the local evaluation map at $v$. Then there is an isomorphism $e$ of simple graphs from `CosetGraph.graph R v n_v` — vertices the cosets $(\mathbb{H}\otimes\mathbb{Q}_v)^\times/\mathrm{level}(R,v)$, where $\mathrm{level}(R,v)$ is generated by [`Submodule.localBoxUnits R v`](def/Submodule_LocalBox.html#L45) together with `scalarUnits v`, and $[x]$ adjacent to $[y]$ when $x\neq y$ and one of them is $[g]$ and the other $[g\,k\,n_v]$ for some $g$ and some $k$ in the level — to the Bruhat–Tits tree of homothety classes of full lattices in $\mathbb{Q}_v^2$ with adjacency generated by `AdjacentLattice`, satisfying $e([g])=\varphi(g)\cdot\mathrm{stdVertex}$ for every unit $g$, where $\varphi(g)\in\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_v)$ acts on lattice classes, and $e([\gamma g])=\varphi(\gamma)\cdot e([g])$ for all units $\gamma,g$.
--
--   This is the local identification, at a prime where the quaternion algebra splits and the order is maximal in the sense fixed by the chart $\varphi$, of the Hecke coset graph attached to a prime Hecke element with the Bruhat–Tits tree of $\mathrm{PGL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_v)$, together with the equivariance of the identification for the left action of the local units. It is used in the further statement computing the rational closure and the cardinalities of dart stabilisers for this graph.
